[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification, so I'm stepping up rather than proceeding.

  [1mUpgrading bounded → architectural.[0m "Persists across the app" and "other forms will need it later" describes a shared
  identity/session layer this repo doesn't have — storage, a read path for other components, a lifetime. That's a new
  subsystem, not a parameter on one stub. The ratchet is one-way, so we'll do the full process: questions, approaches, a
  sectioned design, and a written spec before any code. The signature change to [38;5;153mlogin[39m falls out of that at the end; it
  isn't the work.

  Flagging one thing up front because it shapes the whole design: persisting a user identity client-side is a
  security-posture decision (where it's stored, how long it lives, what an XSS can read, whether it can be forged). I'll
  bring you a real choice on that rather than pick silently.

  First question, and it's the one everything else depends on:

  [1mWhere does the authoritative user identity come from?[0m Right now [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub and [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success: [39m
  [38;5;153mtrue, user: username }[39m without calling anything — so nothing in this codebase has ever received a real ID.

  - [1mA backend issues it[0m — the real login POST returns a user ID (and probably a token/session). The client stores what
    the server gave it. This is the normal shape and makes the identity trustworthy.
  - [1mNo backend yet, but there will be one[0m — we design for the server-issued shape and stub the source, so the storage
    and read paths are real and only the fetch is fake. Nothing to redo later.
  - [1mClient-side only, no backend planned[0m — the app derives an identity itself (e.g. the username, or a UUID minted at
    first login). Works offline, but the identity is self-asserted: any user can claim any ID, so it's usable for
    analytics and personalization, not for authorization.

  Which of these matches where you're headed? If it's the second, I'll also want to know whether the backend's contract
  is already decided or whether we're free to define what login returns.

